## Accessing Tuple Struct Fields by Position in Custom Derive Macros

When working with tuple structs (also called unnamed structs) in a custom derive macro, you need to:

1. **Parse the fields as `syn::Fields::Unnamed`** instead of `syn::Fields::Named`:

```rust
let fields = match input.data {
    syn::Data::Struct(data) => data.fields,
    _ => unimplemented!(),
};

let fields = match fields {
    syn::Fields::Named(ref fields) => &fields.named,
    syn::Fields::Unnamed(ref fields) => &fields.unnamed,
    syn::Fields::Unit => &Default::default(),
};
```

2. **Iterate over fields and use positional indexing** when the field has no identifier:

```rust
let field_prints = fields
    .iter()
    .enumerate()
    .map(|(i, f)| {
        if let Some(field_name) = f.ident.as_ref() {
            // Named field - use the identifier
            quote! {
                println!(" {}: {:?}", stringify!(#field_name), self.#field_name);
            }
        } else {
            // Unnamed (tuple) field - use positional index
            let index = syn::Index::from(i);
            quote! {
                println!(" .{}: {:?}", #index, self.#index);
            }
        }
    })
    .collect::<Vec<_>>();
```

3. **Access tuple struct fields at runtime** using dot notation with their index (`.0`, `.1`, etc.):

```rust
struct Point(i32, i32);
let point = Point(10, 20);
println!("{}", point.0); // Access first field
println!("{}", point.1); // Access second field
```

The key is using `syn::Index::from(i)` to generate the positional accessor (`.0`, `.1`, etc.) and `self.#index` to reference the field in the generated code.

